INT. WATER TOWER, ROOFTOP OF THE MERIDIAN TRUST BUILDING - NIGHT

A cylinder of rusted steel. Pipes sweat in the dark. A single work light clamped to a ladder rung. Forty stories below, city noise rises and flattens to a hum.

PRIYA SHARMA-LOWE (30s, climbing harness, black thermal, headset around her neck) kneels at the base of a pipe, a drill case open beside her. She checks a handheld scanner. Frowns. Checks again.

PRIYA
(to herself)
Okay, Priya. Don't panic. It's just a pipe.

She hefts the drill. Positions it. Stops.

PRIYA (CONT'D)
(to the room, in a stage whisper)
Everyone on the channel, radio check. One-two. One-two.

Static from the headset. Nobody answers. She grimaces and tucks it back around her neck.

PRIYA (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Fine. Solo. Always solo.

The drill whines into the pipe. A thin jet of water hisses sideways. She clamps it with a gloved hand, swears under her breath, plugs the hole with a rag, and wipes her face on her shoulder. The scanner pings. She smiles.

PRIYA (CONT'D)
There you are.

A boot on the ladder. Metal rings. Priya freezes. Slowly, she draws a small flashlight from her belt and clicks it off.

Silence. The boot rings again, closer. A voice, low and amused, floats down.

MARCUS (O.S.)
You always did hate the dark, Pree.

She does not answer. The hatch above swings wide. A pair of boots, then legs, then a grinning man in a charcoal coat descends the ladder, whistling.

MARCUS "SMILEY" ODUYA-FERNANDES (40s, scar through one eyebrow, wire-rimmed glasses) drops the last rung and lands lightly in the puddle. He takes in the drill, the pipe, the rag.

MARCUS
Nice work. Slightly wet.

PRIYA
Marcus.

MARCUS
Priya.

PRIYA
You're supposed to be in Lisbon.

MARCUS
I was. Then I wasn't.

PRIYA
Who told you?

MARCUS
Nobody told me. I read the schematics. Same as you.

PRIYA
(to herself)
Same as me. Great.

MARCUS
Pardon?

PRIYA
Nothing. Talking to the pipe.

MARCUS
Ah. Does the pipe have opinions?

PRIYA
It says you're in my light.

He steps aside, amused. Crouches. Examines the plugged hole.

MARCUS
You drilled the wrong pipe.

PRIYA
I didn't.

MARCUS
The vault line runs behind this one. This is water. Look at the flow.

PRIYA
It reads copper.

MARCUS
It reads copper because someone planted copper filings in the water. Rival crew. They know we're coming.

PRIYA
They know you're coming.

MARCUS
Pree.

PRIYA
What?

MARCUS
Try the next one.

He points. She follows. Another pipe, a hand-span to the left, just as rusty. She aims the scanner. It ticks, ticks, ticks, then one clean tone.

PRIYA
(quietly)
Damn it.

MARCUS
(to the water tower, in a warm, public voice)
Ladies and gentlemen, the lady was right all along, and the gentleman is a fool.

PRIYA
Who are you talking to?

MARCUS
Posterity.

She picks up the drill. Hands it to him. He takes it, surprised.

PRIYA
You drill. I'll hold the flashlight.

MARCUS
I thought you didn't need me.

PRIYA
I don't. But the pipe's behind me and I'm tired of being wet.

He grins. Kneels. The drill whines. Metal screams. A crescent of copper shows through the slot, bright as a coin.

MARCUS
(whispering)
There she is.

PRIYA
(whispering)
Open it.

He pries. The panel swings back. Behind it: a long gray corridor of cables and a steel door stamped MERIDIAN RESERVE.

They look at it together, shoulder to shoulder, breath misting in the dark. Far below, a siren begins, thin and distant.

PRIYA (CONT'D)
That's ours?

MARCUS
Not yet. Pass the cutters.

She passes them. The siren grows louder.
